Emperor
AIR 1946 Bom 533 : (48 Cri LJ 152) (FB) it was held by the Full Bench of the Bombay High Court that the word "proceeding" cannot be Riven such a restricted significance. It is enough if it is made by an inferior criminal court and the order is a judicial order. In several decided cases it was held that the test was not the nature of the proceeding but the nature of the court in which that proceeding was held. See
Emperor v. Devappa Ramappa
(1919) ILR 43 Bom 607 : (1919) 20 Cri LJ 316;
Ram Gopal Goenka v. Corpn. of Calcutta
AIR 1925 Cal 1251 : (1925) 26 Cri LJ 1533);
Kumaravel Nadar v. Shanmuga Nadar
AIR 1940 Mad 465 : ((1940) 41 Cri LJ 769) (FB);
Emperor v. Har Prasad Das
(1913) ILR 40 Cal 477 (FB); In the Matter of the Petition of Bhup Kunwar (1904) ILR 26 All 249 (FB); Karri Adi Reddy, 1972 Mad Lj (Cri.) 512 (Andh. Pra.) and In Re B. G Horniman AIR 1933 Bom 59 : ((1933) 34 Cri LJ 239). With great respect we entirely agree with the view mentioned in those cases that the nature of the court rather than the nature of the proceedings that would determine the issue.
